# PHP 8.1 — fully pinned.
#
# Source: tag 8.1.34 on PHP-8.1-security-backports. The Dockerfile checks out
# the immutable commit in PHP_SRC_COMMIT below (that tag peeled), never the
# branch head.
#
# BASE_IMAGE: trixie by decision -- 8.x and 7.4 take trixie, 7.0-7.3 stay on
# bookworm behind two measured compile walls, and 5.6 stays on bullseye (no
# OpenSSL 1.1 past it). The digest is the one the CI trials built and scanned:
# debian:trixie-slim @ sha256:f6b08b09a05725f9431502d3b5714f5c4576e82c1a84fa1debe0c24a7e0c3ec4
# (multi-arch index carrying linux/amd64 + linux/arm64). Pasted by hand, like every base digest:
# choosing one is the same class of security decision as PHP_SRC_COMMIT, so
# tools/resolve-base-digest.sh prints and never writes. If the trixie-slim tag
# moves later, that is drift -- reported by the tool's --check mode, and
# following it is a reviewed change.
#
# Route: PHP 8.1 -> openssl3. The base must ship OpenSSL 3.x: Debian trixie
# is at 3.5.7, which is why debian:trixie-slim is the base below.
#
# PHP 8.1 supports OpenSSL 3 natively (>= 8.1.0, the build-enforced floor).
#
# Upstream's own last release for this series was 8.1.34 (18 Dec 2025); official
# php/php-src has shipped nothing for it since. The backports fork keeps taking
# fixes from maintained branches, so it is the source of record here -- pinning
# php/php-src instead is a deliberate, reviewed change to the Dockerfile guard,
# not a manifest value.
# PHP_SRC_REPO: all eight series build from this one repository, a fork of
# php/php-src whose per-version branches carry the security backports upstream
# no longer ships for an EOL line. That is why these manifests need no local
# patches/ tree: the patchset IS the pinned branch, not a stack we assemble.
# SOURCE_REF records the release *tag*; PHP_SRC_COMMIT is that tag's peeled
# commit, so the build checks out an immutable object even if the tag were ever
# re-pointed. tools/check-pin-drift.sh reports how far a pin trails upstream.
#
# BUILD_VARIANTS: space-separated list of image variants to produce (cli, fpm).
#
# BUILD_PACKAGES: optional space-separated pinned list of *build-time* packages
# (compiler toolchain + -dev libraries), e.g. "libssl-dev=3.0.15-1~deb12u1".
# Leave empty to use the Dockerfile's per-route defaults. An override replaces
# that whole default list, so it must contain the toolchain and every -dev
# package this version's configure flags need. The openssl CLI is appended
# automatically because the OpenSSL-route assertion depends on it, and the
# effective list is recorded in the image at /opt/php/BUILD-DEPS.
#
# RUNTIME_PACKAGES: optional space-separated pinned list of *runtime* libraries,
# e.g. "libssl3=3.0.15-1~deb12u1 libcurl4 zlib1g libzip4 libmemcached11
# libjpeg62-turbo libpng16-16 libfreetype6 libwebp7 libonig5". Leave empty to
# use the Dockerfile's openssl3 defaults until you pin versions. binutils
# (readelf), unzip (Composer 2 dists) and the openssl CLI are installed
# unconditionally in the runtime stage, so an override only has to list the
# shared libraries the build linked against.
# Some bases name runtime libraries differently (e.g. libwebp7 vs libwebp6);
# confirm actual runtime package names for the chosen base OS.
#
# COMPOSER_VERSION / COMPOSER_SHA256: optional overrides. Leave both empty to
# use the Dockerfile policy pin for this series -- Composer 2.11.0. The phar is
# fetched from an immutable installer commit and verified by SHA-256 at build
# time. If you pin a different release, set COMPOSER_SHA256 alongside it.
#
# SHIP_PHP_SOURCE: 1 (default) ships the patched, configured source/build tree
# in /usr/src/php so downstream images can run docker-php-ext-install, exactly
# like the official PHP images. It is the single largest thing in the image.
# Set 0 for a size-sensitive deployment that will never compile an extension
# inside this image; /usr/src/php then holds only a SOURCE-NOT-SHIPPED note and
# verify-runtime records the choice. The .git history and the PHPT test suites
# are removed either way.
#
# PATCHSET_APPROVED=1 here records one human decision: the upstream patchset
# carried by 8.1.34 is accepted as the reviewed unit (see the "Backports
# patchset" record in manifests/SECURITY-REVIEW.md). It is NOT a claim that
# every open CVE in this series was assessed individually here, and it does
# not survive a pin change -- moving PHP_SRC_COMMIT means a new record. The
# gate still does its job: CI refuses to build a manifest nobody approved.

BASE_IMAGE="debian:trixie-slim@sha256:f6b08b09a05725f9431502d3b5714f5c4576e82c1a84fa1debe0c24a7e0c3ec4"
OPENSSL_ROUTE="openssl3"
PHP_SRC_REPO="https://github.com/shivammathur/php-src-backports.git"
SOURCE_REF="8.1.34"
PHP_SRC_COMMIT="ae65ef4b9ede39ef9923643b741a89c23a2e1c7a"
BUILD_VARIANTS="cli fpm"
# Extensions this series ships ON TOP OF the kit baseline (openssl curl mysqli
# pdo_mysql mbstring zip gd zlib + PECL apcu redis memcached). Empty = baseline only,
# and on this series it is the only valid value: upstream PHP removed ext/mcrypt
# from core in 7.2, so the Dockerfile's availability table refuses it here rather
# than building an image that quietly lacks what the manifest asked for. Setting
# a name anyway is a build error, not a warning.
PHP_EXTENSIONS=""
PECL_EXTENSIONS=""
BUILD_PACKAGES=""
SHIP_PHP_SOURCE="1"
RUNTIME_PACKAGES=""
COMPOSER_VERSION=""
COMPOSER_SHA256=""
PATCHSET_APPROVED="1"
